<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Béhanzin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1845–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Behanzin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Béhanzin was the last independent king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, reigning from 1889 to 1894. He fought two wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> over its claims to the coast around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotonou">Cotonou</a>, but French forces under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-amedee-dodds">Alfred-Amédée Dodds</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> in 1892. He surrendered in 1894 and was exiled to Martinique and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, where he died. He is honoured as a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/behanzin.jpg" alt="Béhanzin" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre d'Albéca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Behanzin-1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Béhanzin, whose name before he took the throne was Kondo, succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glele">Glele</a> after Glele's suicide. His people saw him as intelligent and courageous. Watching the Europeans encroach on the West African coast, he tried to keep them at a distance, and as prince he declined to meet the French envoy Jean Bayol, pleading ritual and ceremonial obligations. He was the last ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> to come to power through its traditional structures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In March 1889 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> attacked a village on the Ouémé whose chief was under French protection; the chief was beheaded and his head wrapped in the tricolour. The French fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotonou">Cotonou</a>, and on 4 March 1890 a Dahomey army of several thousand charged its stockade and was driven off with the loss of several hundred men. At the Battle of Atchoukpa, 350 French soldiers in infantry squares, with King Toffa I's men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>, repelled Béhanzin's force of 9,000. By a treaty of 3 October 1890 Dahomey recognised Porto-Novo as a French protectorate and ceded Cotonou.</p><p>Both sides bought arms, and in 1892 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>'s raids near Grand Popo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to declare war. The French justified annexation by calling the Dahomeans savages, citing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> of slaves at the annual customs and the women soldiers they called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>. General Alfred Dodds advanced up the Ouémé valley from Porto-Novo, and the French lost 85 men to an estimated 2,000 to 4,000 Fon killed. After a failed truce mission on 5 November 1892, Béhanzin burned and abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a>, which the French entered on 17 November, installing his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoli-agbo">Agoli-agbo</a> as king. Unable to rebuild his army, he surrendered on 15 January 1894.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His remains were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> after his death. His throne and his sculptures of wood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> are in the Musée du Quai Branly, and their return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> has been widely discussed. In royal art he is represented by the shark guarding the coast, an egg held in a hand, a pun on his name in Fon, and the smoking pipe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Béhanzin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Behanzin</b>, who succeeded in 1889, rejected French claims, and the First Franco-Dahomean War of 1890 ended in a treaty that recognised French positions on the coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After wars with the Kingdom of Dahomey in 1890 and 1892, General Alfred-Amedee Dodds took Abomey, and <b>King Behanzin</b> surrendered in 1894.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-dahomey">French Dahomey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blida">Blida</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.47,2.83&amp;year=1884" aria-label="Blida on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4621 848 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Béhanzin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5071 1118h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5071 1118h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.47,2.83&amp;year=1884">What was here in 1884? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
